Z||000000||afRaceR||07-24-2001||10:13 PM||||I've had this problem for awhile, and I haven't been able to pin-point the cause.<BR>My hydralic system for the most part is fair, peddle feels weak-sometimes I have to pump the clutch. I'm losing fluid somewhere, can't find where!<BR>Checked the master cylinder-inside &outside, slave cylinder, and the line. Nothing.<BR>The only thing I have noticed is the cap on the master cylinder. the diaphragm thingie doesn't completly seal, not enough to leak though.<BR>Any help. I've got a master cylinder and slave cylinder on the way, but I don't want to have to change them id I don't have to<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||StuGood||07-24-2001||11:55 PM||||I'm no hydraulics wizard by any means, but my favorite way to detect leaks in a line is to: 1) drain the line (disconnect both ends and let the fluid run out), 2) cap one end of the line, and 3) apply a source of compressed air to the other end.  <P>If there's no leak, it'll hold the air pressure for a long time.  Any leaks, and it'll give you a hint, like "Psssst!"  Make it easy to locate.<P>I've done this on fuel lines only.  My guess is that for hydraulics, the principle works the same - except you have to thoroughly flush the lines, before you put them back into service.        ||||reg||1
